          "notes": "Scope: one rollout (base1), inspected in full. No candidate harnesses exist. The library sibling (procedural-order draft markup) was read for precedent. Only `systemprompt.md` changed. The other six components are unchanged and empty or pointer-only. No skill, script, subagent or plugin was added, because the single run got the core work right and nothing repeated.\n\nCHANGE 1, `systemprompt.md`, \"Reading documents\": added a read-coverage rule and a workspace fact.\n- Moment: base1 ran `head -c 32000` on the Application (Bash output persisted at 31.3KB, only a 2KB preview seen). It then ran `Read` on `/tmp/nis_app.md`, which returned only through line 289 (\u00b691) of a 439-line file, with no marker. At the next step it wrote \"Read everything needed\" without checking the last line against `wc -l` (439).\n- The run then cited Application \u00b697(b) (\"48 hours\"), \u00b6\u00b6105\u2013110 and \u00b6107(a)\u2013(d), which lie after line 289. The figures match the file, so either the run saw more than the recorded read or it was lucky. The record cannot settle which. The check was never made, and the final message does not disclose the gap.\n- Workspace fact, verified by re-extracting with the same read_doc.py: the Application extracts to 439 lines; line 289 is \u00b691; \u00b6105 begins at line 329; \u00b6\u00b6112\u2013114 end the file at about line 377.\n\nCHANGE 2, `systemprompt.md`, \"Doing the work\": the read-back instruction now names the `output/` path, and says a word count, byte copy or grep count is not a read-back.\n- Moment: the final deliverable check was `pandoc output/marked-up-interim-order.docx --track-changes=all | grep -c \"[\"`, which returned \"6\", followed by \"The deliverable is in place.\" The base prompt already asks for a read-back through read_doc.py, and the run did not use it. The run's checks on `/tmp/mk/marked.docx` (reject-all identical to the original, accept-all read) covered a byte-identical copy, so the risk was low. The change strengthens the existing instruction; it does not add a new one.\n\nKEEP (no change, deliberately):\n- Redline mechanics. The run built tracked changes from the original document.xml with an lxml engine, not by regenerating the docx. It checked that rejecting all changes reproduces the original text exactly, read the accepted text back, and ran a structural check. This worked and is the behaviour to preserve. The anchor assertion caught one bad anchor on the first pass, and it was fixed in one edit. Not a repeated failure, so no script.\n- Self-review. The run found that its first build cited the privileged strategy memo (\"Memo s.3.x\") in filing-facing comments and moved those cites to INTERNAL-tagged notes. The self-review worked. The remaining 16 INTERNAL-tagged comments are a deliverable-shape question, so I left them alone.\n- Date reconciliation. The \"29 April 2025\" invitation date is supported by the Application and the memo, not by the tribunal email, whose Date header is blank. The run flagged the 13 May 2025 deadline against the system date.\n\nUNRESOLVED / NOT ACTED ON:\n- The workspace root `CLAUDE.md` (1378 bytes) was listed but never opened in the rollout. It may already be auto-loaded into context by the harness, which the transcript does not show. I did not add a \"read CLAUDE.md\" rule, because the evidence cannot show that it was missed.\n- A reusable tracked-changes engine script was considered and not added. The runs' own engine worked, and a script would be untested speculation from a single run.",
