          "notes": "Only one rollout exists (base1), so no edit rests on a divergence. Each change rests on a single-run moment, and the notes say so.\n\n**CHANGED**\n\n1. **New skill `skills/interim-order-redline/SKILL.md`, with trigger line in `systemprompt.md`** (the trigger is under \"Doing the work\", naming `interim-order-redline` verbatim). The skill covers the controlling sources, the measure-by-measure test, finding-to-order consistency, concessions in replacement wording, forum limits, cross-instrument references, privilege placement, pre-finish checks, and the final-message order. Workspace facts are labelled \"In this workspace\". Moments behind it:\n   - **Finding-order contradiction.** Deliverable text, lines 67\u201371: \u00b64.3 says the Tribunal \"is not satisfied\" there is a real dissipation risk, \u00b64.4 says element (c) fails for the asset order, and \u00b65 still orders a USD 47.5m freeze (\"Accordingly, the Tribunal hereby orders\u2026\"). Nothing in the run checked that each operative paragraph still rests on a standing finding. This is \u00a73.\n   - **Unflagged concession.** Deliverable \u00b64.1 (line 63) has the Tribunal provisionally satisfied that the Claimant established a prima facie case that NIS \"failed to deliver\". PO1 \u00b63 records NIS denying breach and saying shortfalls \"to the extent they occurred\" were excused. The final message does not flag this. This is \u00a74.\n   - **Forum and \u00a714.4 analysis.** Base1 caught that the Bogot\u00e1 body is described as a \"Tribunal de Arbitraje\" and that \u00a714.4 may not reach it. The skill encodes that check and adds the Application \u00b674 pleading that the filing breaches \u00a714.2. Base1 already covered the core point, so this section is partly a restatement and could be cut.\n   - **Source conflicts.** PO1 \u00b63 cites \"Section 9\" of the SOA for force majeure; SOA \u00a78 is force majeure, and the Application cites \u00a78. Base1 did not list this in its source-conflicts section. Counsel address conflicts (PO1 \u00b610, draft recital 2 and \u00b618, Application counsel block, memo signature) were partly handled by base1, and the skill records them as workspace facts.\n\n2. **`guardrails.md` (was empty): one line on rule text.** Base1's final message says \"The cross-undertaking should rest on PO1 \u00b615, not ICC Art. 28(1) as the memo says\" (rollout ~line 1954). That is a negative claim about a rule whose text is not in the file, which the library guardrail also forbids. The line is universal to this practice area, so it is always-on.\n\n3. **Internal notes kept out of the file (skill \u00a78 and \u00a79).** Rollout ~lines 1912\u20131915 and 1965: the file contains 16 comments tagged \"INTERNAL \u2013 delete before filing\", and the final message asks the lawyer to strip them before filing. This is a judgment call that I made, not a failure the run showed. The rule is that internal analysis goes in the final message, so the file needs no repair.\n\n4. **Per-change grounds in the final message (skill \u00a79, item f).** The assessed text (deliverable txt, same reader as the grader) shows accepted text only. Comment text is absent (grep finds none of the comment strings) and deleted text is absent. Base1's final message gives decisions but not a per-change ground. Because comments are invisible to this reader, item (f) is the only readable place for the grounds. The library EXPERIENCE for a different task records the same comment-visibility risk. I did not copy that task's skill, because its trigger (procedural order) does not match an interim order.\n\n**KEEP_BASE (unchanged)**\n- `LongTermMEMORY.md`: left empty on purpose. No matter facts were stored as durable memory.\n- `scripts/`, `plugin/`, `tool_descriptions/`, `skills/README.md`: no trajectory evidence for a change. Base1 built its tracked-change engine by hand and it produced a correct reject-all match, so I did not add a script.\n- Traceability bullet in `systemprompt.md`: base1 bracketed the disputed dates and the Respondent address, and it did not assert unsupported facts, so no edit.\n- Verification bullet: base1 read the accepted text back in full (rollout ~l.1637\u20131815), which is what the bullet asks for.\n\n**NOT VERIFIED / OPEN**\n- Base1's final-message claims that Application \u00b697(b) gives a 48-hour force majeure notice period and that \u00b659 covers interest, costs and expenses. I did not find support for either in the Application text I could read, so neither is in the harness.\n- The workspace `CLAUDE.md` (1,378 bytes, at the workspace root) was never opened by base1. Its contents are not in my context, so I cannot say whether it contains binding instructions. Someone should check it.\n- The skill's \u00a79 \"in this run\" date fact (system date 19 October 2026 against a 13 May 2025 deadline) is a per-run observation and should not carry over to other matters.",
