          "notes": "revised -> systemprompt.md \"Reading documents\": replaced inv1's check-then-cite rule with a chunked-read rule (files over about 150 lines are read with offset/limit or sed until the last line; no citing unread paragraphs) -> base1's Read of the Application stopped at line 289 (\u00b691) of 439 with no marker (rollout ~l.1153\u20131154); the next message says \"Read everything needed\" (~l.1444), yet the final message and deliverable cite \u00b697(b), \u00b6101 and \u00b6\u00b6105\u2013110, which no read observation in base1 contains. inv1 hit the same cut at line 289 (~l.1355) even with the check-form rule in its prompt; it ran wc -l (439) and still did not read on. dom1 read the Application by offset/limit (~l.1224\u20131225) and did not hit the cut. 2 of 2 whole-file readers were cut; the chunked form is the one that worked (1/1, dom1). The record cannot show whether the run saw the paragraphs it cited; the citations match the source.\n\ndropped -> inv1 systemprompt workspace fact (Application = 439 lines; line 289 = \u00b691; \u00b6105 at line 329) -> matter-specific always-on text that costs budget on every run and has no use for other matters; the same moment is covered by the general chunked-read rule.\n\ndropped -> inv1 read-back wording (\"a word count, byte copy or grep count is not a read-back\") -> base1 checked the output by grep count (~l.1919) and inv1 by grep count on readback.txt (~l.1592), but both had already read the accepted text in full (base1 ~l.1637; inv1 ~l.1472). No defect traced to the count check. The library entry for the same wording records no observed effect. Base wording kept.\n\nkept -> systemprompt.md \"Doing the work\" grounding bullet and read-back line (base wording) -> all three rollouts delivered output/marked-up-interim-order.docx and reject-all matched the source in each (base1 ~l.1624; inv1 readback; dom1 \"reject-all paragraphs 107, mismatches 0\"). No nothing-produced case, and no evidence for a change.\n\nadded -> systemprompt.md trigger line naming the `interim-order-redline` skill (dom1) -> dom1 invoked the skill as its work moved through the findings and concessions, and it is the only run that avoided the \u00b64.1 concession (see the next line). Evidence is 1 rollout; the trigger is needed for the skill to load at all, and the name appears verbatim in the always-on text.\n\nkept (skill \u00a74, revised form) -> skills/interim-order-redline/SKILL.md \u00a74: the file carries the least concessive wording that still does what the instruction asks, and the instruction's own wording and the concession it makes go in the final message -> the strategy memo \u00a73.1 instructs the replacement \"the Tribunal is provisionally satisfied that the Claimant has established a prima facie case on the merits\" (a concession on breach). base1 filed it in \u00b64.1 (\"provisionally satisfied \u2026 failed to deliver\") and its final message does not name the concession; inv1 filed a sharper version (\"did not deliver 31,200 MT\", \u00b64.1) and does not name it; dom1 recorded the allegation and the denial, and flagged the memo wording as decision 2. 2 of 3 rollouts filed the concession. Choice of default: a filed concession is hard to withdraw, so the file holds the least concessive text and the lawyer chooses the memo wording.\n\nkept -> skill \u00a73 findings-to-orders consistency -> base1 accepted text has \u00b64.3 \"not satisfied \u2026 real risk \u2026 dissipate\", \u00b64.4 element (c) not satisfied for the asset order, then \u00b65 freezing USD 47.5m and \"Accordingly, the Tribunal hereby orders\" (deliverable txt ~l.61\u201375). The contradiction is unflagged in base1's final message (it only says \"leading with 'no asset measure' is your call\"). inv1 has \u00b64.3 \"no finding on dissipation\" with \u00b64.4 \"satisfied \u2026 measures are warranted\" over the same freeze. dom1 deleted the \u00b64.3 finding, made the freeze depend on \u00b64.4 placeholders, and flagged it as the main decision. Two of three rollouts show the defect.\n\nkept -> skill \u00a78 no internal notes in the Tribunal-facing file -> base1 left 16 comments tagged \"INTERNAL \u2013 delete before filing\" in the file and asks the lawyer to strip them (final message; build script INT tag). inv1 and dom1 kept internal analysis out of the file.\n\nrevised -> skill \u00a77 rule discipline: filed text carries at most a short bracket; reasons and the unverified rule list go in the final message -> dom1's wording put \"[rule text to be verified]\" into comments, which conflicts with the area's library guardrail (procedural-order entry: \"say so in your final message, not in the filed document\"). No run in this set filed an unsupported rule claim: base1's and inv1's Article 28(1) statements rest on the Application \u00b642 quotation, and the SIAA s.12(1) and Cap. 143A recital is in Application \u00b644. The change is for consistency with the area, not a demonstrated fix.\n\ndropped -> dom1 guardrails.md line (\"Do not state what a rule \u2026 says \u2026 unless its text is in the file\") -> no run in this set made an unsupported rule claim that the line would have stopped. base1 (final: \"SIAA text and the Singapore \u00a712(6) reference \u2026 not in the file\") and inv1 (final: \"could not check the Singapore IAA citations\") already flagged unverified rule points. The line duplicates the skill \u00a77 discipline and its filed-document placement conflicts with the library. KEEP_BASE for guardrails.md (empty).\n\nrevised -> skill \u00a79 final-message order: each item kept short -> dom1's final message stops mid-item (b) in the record (\"decides w\"), so items (c)\u2013(f) are absent from the trajectory. The cause is not visible. base1 and inv1 finished their final messages. The shorter form reduces the risk, but the cause is unconfirmed.\n\nrevised -> skill \u00a79 deadline check: per-run date fact removed, kept as the general rule \"compare the system date with the response deadline; if passed, say so\" -> dom1's \"13 May 2025 vs 19 October 2026\" is a per-run observation. base1 and inv1 also flagged the lapsed deadline in their finals, so the rule is supported across runs.\n\nkept (as a labelled section) -> skill \u00a710 matter notes (PO1 \u00b615 elements and cross-undertaking; SOA \u00a714.4 text and forum gap; PO1 \u00b63 \"Section 9\" vs Section 8; counsel-address conflicts; quantum 54,000 \u00d7 879.63 = 47,500,020) -> each fact checked against the source (PO1 \u00b63, SOA 14.4, Application \u00b674, Application \u00b644, memo signature). Labelled \"verify before reuse\" because it is loaded only on trigger and applies to this matter only.\n\ndropped -> dom1 skill \u00a71\u2013\u00a76 \"In this workspace\" labels, moved to \u00a710 -> generic rules kept in \u00a71\u2013\u00a79; matter facts isolated so later matters do not inherit them.\n\nKEEP_BASE -> scripts/ (none added) -> base1 hand-built the tracked-changes engine and verified reject-all identity; inv1 and dom1 did the same. No repeated mechanical failure, so no script.\n\nKEEP_BASE -> plugin/ and hooks.json (none added) -> all three rollouts delivered; no in-loop enforcement need shown.\n\nKEEP_BASE -> sub_agents/ (none) -> the input is not large relative to the answer; the work is one document and one judgment.\n\nKEEP_BASE -> LongTermMEMORY.md (empty) and tool_descriptions/tool_guidance.md (unchanged) -> no durable matter fact or tool-use failure recurs across runs.\n\nUnresolved (not acted on) -> workspace CLAUDE.md (1,378 bytes in base1; 2,198 bytes in inv1's listing) -> not opened by any run; its contents are unknown, so no rule was added.",
